e vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Each non-sunny line can cover many points. The goal is to cover all points with n lines. The total number of points is n(n+1)/2. Each sunny line can cover at most n points (one per column). So total coverage capacity of sunny lines is at most k*n. Each non-sunny line can cover at most n points as well (vertical lines cover up to n points, horizontal lines cover up to n points, slope -1 lines cover up to n points). So total coverage capacity of all lines is at most n*k + (n-k)*n = n*n = n^2. But we need to cover n(n+1)/2 points, which is less than n^2 for n≥2. So capacity is not a limiting factor.\n\nBut the constraints about column coverage are more restrictive. Let's consider column i (1 ≤ i ≤ n). Column i has h_i = n+1-i points. Sunny lines can cover at most k points in column i (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column). Non-sunny lines can cover at most (n-k) points in column i, but each non-sunny line can cover at most one point per column unless it's vertical. So the total number of points in column i that can be covered by non-vertical non-sunny lines is at most (n-k) (one per line). However, vertical lines can cover all points in a column. So we need to allocate vertical lines to columns where we need to cover many points beyond the capacity of sunny lines.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: we need to assign vertical lines to some columns, and the rest of the columns must be covered by sunny lines (and possibly horizontal or slope -1 lines) such that each column's points are covered.\n\nBut horizontal and slope -1 lines also can cover at most one point per column each, similar to sunny lines. So the only way to cover more than (k + (n-k)) = n points per column is to have a vertical line in that column. Since each column i has h_i points, we need to ensure that for each column i, either we have a vertical line (cover all h_i points) or we have enough non-vertical lines (sunny + horizontal + slope -1) to c