vertical lines for columns i = 1,...,|V|. So the set V must be the first |V| columns (the leftmost columns). So we must place vertical lines in the leftmost columns.\n\nThus the number of vertical lines needed is at least the smallest integer t such that for all columns i > t, h_i ≤ n - t. Since h_i = n+1-i, we need n+1-i ≤ n - t => 1 - i ≤ -t => i ≥ t+1. So any column i ≥ t+1 satisfies the condition. So we need vertical lines for columns i = 1,...,t. So t is the number of vertical lines needed.\n\nNow we have n - k non-sunny lines total, which includes vertical lines plus possibly horizontal or slope -1 lines. But vertical lines are a subset of non-sunny lines. So the number of vertical lines V ≤ n - k. So we can have at most n - k vertical lines. So t ≤ n - k.\n\nThus we need to find t such that t ≤ n - k and t is the minimal number of vertical lines needed to satisfy the condition. Actually we need to ensure that for columns i > t, h_i ≤ n - t. Let's compute t minimal such that for all i > t, n+1-i ≤ n - t => i ≥ t+1 => condition holds automatically for i > t. So any t works? Wait, we need to ensure that for columns i > t, the condition holds. Let's check for i = t+1: h_{t+1} = n+1-(t+1) = n - t. So h_{t+1} = n - t, which is exactly the number of remaining lines (non-vertical). So column t+1 can be covered by the remaining lines, each covering one point. For i > t+1, h_i = n+1-i n - t, so also okay. So the condition is satisfied for any t such that we have vertical lines for columns 1,...,t. So the minimal number of vertical lines needed is t = max{ i such that h_i > n - (number of non-vertical lines) }? Let's derive more systematically.\n\nWe have total lines n. Among them, k are sunny, n - k are non-sunny. Among non-sunny lines, we can have v vertical lines, and the rest (n - k - v) are horizontal or slope -1 lines. Horizontal and slope -1 lines also can cover at most one point per column each. So the total number of lines that can cover at most one point per c