vers one point in each of these columns). So this suggests that any k is possible? Let's test.\n\nIf we set v = n - k vertical lines covering columns 1,...,n - k, then each column i > n - k has h_i = n+1-i points. For i = n - k + 1, h_i = n+1-(n - k + 1) = k. For i = n - k + 2, h_i = k - 1, etc. So the columns to the right have decreasing number of points: k, k-1, ..., 1. So we have k sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we can assign each sunny line to cover one point in each of the rightmost k columns. For column i = n - k + j (j from 1 to k), there are exactly k+1-j points. So we need to cover those points with k sunny lines. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, we can cover at most k points in column i (since we have k sunny lines). But column i has at most k points (since j≥1). So we can cover them. However, we need to ensure that the points we assign to each sunny line are distinct across columns (i.e., each sunny line picks one point per column). Since each column has at most k points, we can assign each sunny line to a distinct point in each column. But we need to ensure that the points selected across columns lie on the same line (sunny line). That's the tricky part: we cannot arbitrarily assign points to a sunny line; they must be collinear.\n\nThus the above capacity argument is necessary but not sufficient. The existence of a set of k sunny lines that cover the rightmost columns with the required points is not guaranteed. The solution's construction for k=3 uses three sunny lines that each pass through two points in the rightmost columns, forming a pattern. For k=1, they used a sunny line covering the single point (n,1). For k=0, no sunny lines.\n\nFor k=2, we would need to find two sunny lines that together cover all points in the rightmost columns (columns n-1 and n). Let's examine the rightmost columns for general n.\n\nRightmost columns: column n has 1 point (n,1). Column n-1 has 2 points: (