d (5,1). Equation: y-2 = 1*(x-6) => y = x - 4. At x=4, y = 0, not allowed. At x=7, y = 3, but (7,3) not in T_7 (7+3=10 >8). So L3 covers (6,2) and (5,1). Good.\n\nNow remaining points: (5,3),(4,1),(4,3),(4,4). That's 4 points left, with 1 sunny line left. Can we cover these 4 points with a single sunny line? No, because a line can cover at most one point per column, and we have points in columns 5 and 4 only. Actually (5,3) is column 5, (4,1),(4,3),(4,4) are column 4. So we have 2 columns: column 5 has 1 point left, column 4 has 3 points left. A single line can cover at most one point in column 4, so cannot cover all three. So this fails.\n\nThus this particular assignment fails.\n\nWe need a more systematic approach. Perhaps we can try to use lines that each cover two points in column 4 and one point in column 5, etc. But each line can only have one point per column, so a line cannot cover two points in column 4. So we need at least 3 lines to cover the 3 points in column 4 (since each line can cover at most one point in column 4). Since we have 4 lines total, we can allocate 3 lines to cover the 3 points in column 4, and the remaining line to cover the points in columns 5,6,7. But we also need to cover points in columns 5,6,7. Let's see.\n\nColumn 4 has points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4) (4 points). Actually column 4 has 4 points. So we need at least 4 lines to cover them, because each line can cover at most one point in column 4. Since we have exactly 4 sunny lines, each must cover exactly one point in column 4. So each sunny line must include a distinct point from column 4. So we need to assign each of the 4 points in column 4 to a distinct sunny line. Then each sunny line also must cover points in columns 5,6,7 (maybe). So we need to find 4 lines, each passing through a distinct point in column 4, and collectively covering all points in columns 5,6,7.\n\nThus we need to assign each point in column 4 to a line, and then for each line, we need to pick at most one poi