x = n-3, y = 1 -0.5*(n-3 - n) = 1 -0.5*(-3) = 1 + 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L1 covers only those two points among the set.\n\nNow we need to cover the remaining points: (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4), (n-2,1),(n-2,3), (n-1,1),(n-1,2). That's 8 points left. We have 3 sunny lines left. Each line can cover at most one point per column. So each line can cover at most 4 points (one per each of the four columns). So total capacity is 12 points, enough for 8. So we need to find 3 lines that cover these 8 points.\n\nWe need to assign points to lines such that each line picks at most one per column. Let's try to find a systematic way.\n\nWe can treat columns as categories: column n-3 (C1), column n-2 (C2), column n-1 (C3), column n (C4). We have 4 columns. We need to assign each point to a line such that each line gets at most one point from each column. This is like a bipartite matching problem: we have lines as bins, each can hold at most one point from each column. Since we have 4 lines total, we can assign each column's points across lines.\n\nLet's list points per column:\n\nC1 (n-3): (n-3,1), (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-3,4) (4 points)\nC2 (n-2): (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3) (3 points)\nC3 (n-1): (n-1,1), (n-1,2) (2 points)\nC4 (n): (n,1) (1 point)\n\nWe already assigned (n,1) and (n-2,2) to L1. So C4 is done, C2 has (n-2,2) assigned. So remaining points:\n\nC1: 4 points\nC2: (n-2,1), (n-2,3) (2 points)\nC3: 2 points\nC4: none left.\n\nWe have 3 lines left (L2, L3, L4). Each line can take at most one point from each column. So we need to assign the remaining points to these lines.\n\nWe have 4 points in C1, 2 in C2, 2 in C3. That's total 8 points. With 3 lines, each line can take at most 1 from C1, 1 from C2, 1 from C3. So each line can take at most 3 points (one per column). So total capacity is 9 points, enough for 8. So we need to assign points such that each line gets at most one per column.\n\nOne possible assignment:\n\n- L2: (n-3,1) from C1, (n-2,1) from C2, (n-1,1) fro