umns 1,...,n-2. Could we use a horizontal line y=1 to cover (n,1) and also other points? Horizontal line y=1 is non-sunny (parallel to x-axis). So we could use a horizontal line y=1 as one of the non-sunny lines. But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines, which we used as vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2. However, we could replace one of those vertical lines with a horizontal line y=1. But then column i for that vertical line would be uncovered. But maybe we can cover that column's points using sunny lines. Let's think.\n\nWe have n lines total, 2 sunny, n-2 non-sunny. We need to cover all points. Let's try to allocate non-sunny lines as follows: Use vertical lines for columns 1,..., n-3 (i.e., n-3 vertical lines). Then we have 3 columns left: columns n-2, n-1, n. We have n-2 non-sunny lines total, so we have used n-3 vertical lines, leaving 1 non-sunny line left. That remaining non-sunny line could be horizontal or diagonal. Then we have 2 sunny lines. So we need to cover the points in columns n-2, n-1, n using 1 non-sunny line + 2 sunny lines.\n\nLet's examine the points in those columns:\n\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Since a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n+1-(n-2) = 3. So column n-2 has 3 points.\n\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1), (n-1,2). (b ≤ 2)\n\n- Column n: point (n,1). (b ≤ 1)\n\nTotal points in these three columns: 3+2+1 = 6 points.\n\nWe have 3 lines to cover them: 1 non-sunny line (vertical/horizontal/diagonal) and 2 sunny lines. Each line can cover at most one point per column (except vertical lines can cover all points in a column). So we need to allocate coverage.\n\nIf the non-sunny line is vertical, say x = n-2, it would cover all 3 points in column n-2. Then we need to cover the 3 points in columns n-1 and n with 2 sunny lines. As before, we have points (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). Two sunny lines can each intersect each column at most once. So we can assign one sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and (n,1) (but that would be a line through (n-1,1) and (n,1