umns) have many points, so we likely need vertical lines for those columns. The rightmost columns have few points (1,2,3,...). So we can cover them with non-vertical lines.\n\nThus, the maximum number of sunny lines is limited by the number of columns that have at most one point each (or maybe two points). Let's examine.\n\nColumn a has n+1-a points. So column n has 1 point, column n-1 has 2 points, column n-2 has 3 points, column n-3 has 4 points, etc.\n\nIf we use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (i.e., n-3 vertical lines), we have used n-3 lines, leaving 3 lines to cover columns n-2, n-1, n. The points in those columns are:\n\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3) (3 points)\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1), (n-1,2) (2 points)\n- Column n: point (n,1) (1 point)\n\nTotal points left: 3+2+1 = 6 points. We have 3 lines left. Each line can cover at most one point per column. So each line can cover at most 3 points (one in each of the three columns). So with 3 lines we can cover at most 9 points, which is enough for 6 points. However, we need to ensure that the lines are distinct and can be arranged to cover all points. Since we have 3 lines, we can try to assign each line to cover one point in each column. For example, line L1 covers (n-2,1), (n-1,1), (n,1). That's three points, one per column. L2 covers (n-2,2), (n-1,2), maybe some other point? But column n has only (n,1) which is already covered. So L2 could cover (n-2,2) and maybe some other point in column n-1? Actually (n-1,2) is already covered by L2. L3 covers (n-2,3) maybe and something else. So we can cover all points with 3 lines, each covering one point per column. So we can have 3 lines that are not vertical. They could be sunny lines or non-sunny lines (horizontal or slope -1). So we could have up to 3 sunny lines if we choose them all to be sunny. So k=3 is possible.\n\nNow can we have k=4? That would require at least 4 sunny lines. Since we have n lines total, we need to allocate at most n-4 